1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,214
This spring, the boss of Starbucks said something

2
00:00:03,214 --> 00:00:05,830
you don't often hear from a chief executive.

3
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:07,912
"In some cases," Brian Niccol said,

4
00:00:07,912 --> 00:00:11,420
"a nine dollar experience does feel like you're splurging."

5
00:00:11,870 --> 00:00:16,148
Nine dollars is about what the average customer spends on a visit.

6
00:00:16,148 --> 00:00:18,870
A latte, maybe a sandwich, maybe a pastry.

7
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:22,284
Now here is the strange part.

8
00:00:22,284 --> 00:00:28,827
In a grande latte, the coffee itself costs about thirteen cents.

9
00:00:28,827 --> 00:00:31,076
The milk, maybe forty.

10
00:00:31,076 --> 00:00:34,960
The cup and the lid, a few cents more.

11
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,720
Less than a dollar, all in.

12
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:39,610
So where does the rest of your money go?

13
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:41,700
We don't have to guess.

14
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:46,476
Starbucks just closed its books on fiscal 2026: almost thirty eight

15
00:00:46,476 --> 00:00:51,680
billion dollars in sales, from more than forty one thousand coffeehouses.

16
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,460
Every dollar is in there, line by line.

17
00:00:54,910 --> 00:00:57,830
So let's take a nine dollar order, and take it apart.

18
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:03,171
Start with the price tag.

19
00:01:03,171 --> 00:01:08,671
A grande caffè latte costs around five forty-five, depending on where you live.

20
00:01:08,671 --> 00:01:12,710
Add a breakfast sandwich, and you're at nine dollars fast.

21
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,422
And it keeps going up.

22
00:01:14,422 --> 00:01:17,346
In its last quarter, the average American ticket at

23
00:01:17,346 --> 00:01:20,099
Starbucks rose more than five percent in a year.

24
00:01:20,099 --> 00:01:24,400
Across the country, coffee prices are up forty seven percent in five years.

25
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:26,384
It's not just Starbucks.

26
00:01:26,384 --> 00:01:29,773
In January, the government's measure of coffee prices

27
00:01:29,773 --> 00:01:32,778
was eighteen percent higher than a year before.

28
00:01:32,778 --> 00:01:36,230
By the end of 2025, the typical plain hot coffee in an

29
00:01:36,230 --> 00:01:40,130
American café already cost three dollars and sixty one cents.

30
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:44,336
Starbucks says people are still coming, and it's true.

31
00:01:44,336 --> 00:01:49,762
Transactions in the United States rose more than three percent in fiscal 2026.

32
00:01:49,762 --> 00:01:54,492
Niccol's explanation is that when you give people something special,

33
00:01:54,492 --> 00:01:57,900
"a little touch of luxury," it "goes a long way."

34
00:01:58,350 --> 00:02:02,931
So the obvious guess is that you're paying for expensive coffee.

35
00:02:02,931 --> 00:02:03,790
Let's check.

36
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:07,189
Coffee beans did get expensive.

37
00:02:07,189 --> 00:02:11,640
In February 2025, the price of arabica coffee on the futures

38
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:16,387
market hit a record, about four dollars and forty cents a pound.

39
00:02:16,387 --> 00:02:20,170
Bad weather in Brazil and Vietnam had cut harvests.

40
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:22,757
But a pound goes a long way.

41
00:02:22,757 --> 00:02:26,190
A hot grande latte has two shots of espresso.

42
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:29,853
That's roughly eighteen grams of roasted coffee.

43
00:02:29,853 --> 00:02:33,592
At today's price, about two seventy-five a pound,

44
00:02:33,592 --> 00:02:36,568
that's around thirteen cents of coffee.

45
00:02:36,568 --> 00:02:40,460
Even at the record high, it was about twenty cents.

46
00:02:40,910 --> 00:02:42,746
The milk is the bigger ingredient.

47
00:02:42,746 --> 00:02:45,229
There's about twelve ounces of it in a grande.

48
00:02:45,229 --> 00:02:48,037
At the average supermarket price of four dollars and

49
00:02:48,037 --> 00:02:50,952
ten cents a gallon, that's roughly thirty eight cents.

50
00:02:50,952 --> 00:02:52,950
Starbucks almost certainly pays less.

51
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:58,279
Then the cup, the lid and the sleeve: a small coffee shop told

52
00:02:58,279 --> 00:03:03,000
Marketplace those cost between four and seven cents a drink.

53
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:09,075
Add it up, and the ingredients in a five dollar latte come to around sixty cents.

54
00:03:09,075 --> 00:03:11,089
Our estimate, but a fair one.

55
00:03:11,089 --> 00:03:13,450
About eleven percent of the price.

56
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:15,237
And notice something.

57
00:03:15,237 --> 00:03:18,993
Since that record, arabica has fallen by a third in a year.

58
00:03:18,993 --> 00:03:21,158
Menu prices didn't follow it down.

59
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:24,660
That tells you the beans were never what set the price.

60
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,474
So let's go to the books.

61
00:03:27,474 --> 00:03:30,558
For every dollar Starbucks took in last year,

62
00:03:30,558 --> 00:03:35,356
thirty two cents went to what it calls product and distribution costs.

63
00:03:35,356 --> 00:03:38,920
On a nine dollar order, that's about two ninety-one.

64
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:42,922
That's far more than the coffee and milk in your latte,

65
00:03:42,922 --> 00:03:46,474
because it covers everything Starbucks sells and ships.

66
00:03:46,474 --> 00:03:51,318
The sandwiches and the pastries, which cost much more to make than a drink.

67
00:03:51,318 --> 00:03:53,514
The cups and lids, by the billion.

68
00:03:53,514 --> 00:03:57,970
The roasting plants, the warehouses and the trucks that carry it all.

69
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:03,460
It also covers the coffee and supplies Starbucks sells to other companies.

70
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:08,295
About two thirds of its coffeehouses are run by licensees, in airports,

71
00:04:08,295 --> 00:04:12,721
in grocery stores, and now in China, where Starbucks sold control

72
00:04:12,721 --> 00:04:16,603
of its stores this spring and kept a forty percent stake.

73
00:04:16,603 --> 00:04:19,940
Those partners buy their products from Starbucks,

74
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:22,460
at much thinner margins than a latte.

75
00:04:22,910 --> 00:04:26,099
So the drink in your hand is the cheapest thing Starbucks makes.

76
00:04:26,099 --> 00:04:28,790
The expensive part is the building you're standing in.

77
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:34,825
The biggest line in the whole company is the cost of running the coffeehouses.

78
00:04:34,825 --> 00:04:36,913
Forty five cents of every dollar.

79
00:04:36,913 --> 00:04:39,570
Four dollars and eight cents of your nine.

80
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:44,207
Look only at the stores Starbucks runs itself, and it's even bigger.

81
00:04:44,207 --> 00:04:48,332
Running them ate more than fifty five percent of what they took in.

82
00:04:48,332 --> 00:04:53,380
Spend nine dollars in one, and about five dollars goes to keeping that store open.

83
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:55,625
Most of that is two things.

84
00:04:55,625 --> 00:04:58,550
The people behind the counter, and the rent.

85
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,062
Starbucks employs about two hundred and fourteen

86
00:05:02,062 --> 00:05:05,316
thousand people in its American coffeehouses alone.

87
00:05:05,316 --> 00:05:09,846
In April, the company said its baristas and shift supervisors earn more

88
00:05:09,846 --> 00:05:14,440
than thirty dollars an hour on average, once you count pay and benefits.

89
00:05:14,890 --> 00:05:17,248
And Niccol's plan has added more of them.

90
00:05:17,248 --> 00:05:21,732
Back to Starbucks spent five hundred million dollars on staffing and training,

91
00:05:21,732 --> 00:05:25,930
so that there are enough hands behind the bar when the morning rush hits.

92
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:28,862
This year, it added bonuses of up to twelve hundred

93
00:05:28,862 --> 00:05:31,450
dollars a year, and tipping on mobile orders.

94
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:34,316
Here's why that matters for the price.

95
00:05:34,316 --> 00:05:37,176
Wages don't go down when coffee gets cheaper.

96
00:05:37,176 --> 00:05:38,320
Neither does rent.

97
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:40,100
These costs only go one way.

98
00:05:40,550 --> 00:05:42,395
Then there is the room itself.

99
00:05:42,395 --> 00:05:47,438
Starbucks is remodeling its stores, at around a hundred and fifty thousand dollars

100
00:05:47,438 --> 00:05:51,990
each, and it planned well over a thousand of them by the end of this year.

101
00:05:51,990 --> 00:05:55,188
That's the "experience" in a nine dollar experience.

102
00:05:55,188 --> 00:05:56,910
You pay for it in every cup.

103
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:00,999
After the store, the money moves upstairs.

104
00:06:00,999 --> 00:06:05,126
Fifty seven cents of your nine dollars goes to general and

105
00:06:05,126 --> 00:06:09,325
administrative costs: the head office, technology, the app.

106
00:06:09,325 --> 00:06:13,452
Thirty five cents covers the slow wearing out of equipment

107
00:06:13,452 --> 00:06:17,010
and buildings, what accountants call depreciation.

108
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:21,915
And about twenty nine cents went to restructuring and other costs.

109
00:06:21,915 --> 00:06:24,345
Turning a company around isn't free.

110
00:06:24,345 --> 00:06:29,812
In 2025, Starbucks closed more than six hundred coffeehouses in a single quarter,

111
00:06:29,812 --> 00:06:33,660
and closing a store means paying to get out of its lease.

112
00:06:34,110 --> 00:06:35,005
Add it all up.

113
00:06:35,005 --> 00:06:38,458
Of your nine dollars, about eight dollars and thirteen

114
00:06:38,458 --> 00:06:41,910
cents is gone before Starbucks makes a cent of profit.

115
00:06:43,010 --> 00:06:45,795
What's left is the operating profit.

116
00:06:45,795 --> 00:06:50,436
In fiscal 2026, it was nine point seven cents on the dollar.

117
00:06:50,436 --> 00:06:53,530
On your order, about eighty seven cents.

118
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:57,380
That's up from seven point nine percent the year before.

119
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:00,293
Niccol says the turnaround is now a growth plan.

120
00:07:00,293 --> 00:07:04,300
But it's a long way from a business printing money on cheap beans.

121
00:07:04,750 --> 00:07:07,991
In North America, where most of the money is made,

122
00:07:07,991 --> 00:07:11,298
it did a little better: twelve cents on the dollar.

123
00:07:11,298 --> 00:07:14,150
Still about a dollar on a nine dollar order.

124
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,178
Then come the bankers and the taxman.

125
00:07:17,178 --> 00:07:20,801
Interest on Starbucks's debt takes about nine cents.

126
00:07:20,801 --> 00:07:25,051
A one-time gain from the China sale adds back about thirteen.

127
00:07:25,051 --> 00:07:27,280
And taxes take thirty one cents.

128
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:32,422
That leaves about sixty cents of profit on a nine dollar order.

129
00:07:32,422 --> 00:07:34,210
Six point seven percent.

130
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:39,187
That's an average across the whole company, every latte, every sandwich,

131
00:07:39,187 --> 00:07:42,897
every bag of beans sold to a supermarket, in every country.

132
00:07:42,897 --> 00:07:46,104
A plain drink in a busy store probably does better.

133
00:07:46,104 --> 00:07:48,682
A slow store in a pricey mall does worse.

134
00:07:48,682 --> 00:07:50,380
But that's the shape of it.

135
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:53,655
So here is the full receipt.

136
00:07:53,655 --> 00:07:57,694
Four dollars and eight cents to run the coffeehouse.

137
00:07:57,694 --> 00:08:00,879
Two ninety-one for products and shipping.

138
00:08:00,879 --> 00:08:06,706
A dollar fourteen for the head office, the equipment and the restructuring.

139
00:08:06,706 --> 00:08:10,900
About twenty seven cents, net, for interest and taxes.

140
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:12,920
And sixty cents of profit.

141
00:08:13,370 --> 00:08:17,730
The coffee beans are a rounding error inside the second line.

142
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:22,828
That's why your latte didn't get cheaper when coffee fell by a third.

143
00:08:22,828 --> 00:08:25,388
Starbucks isn't really selling coffee.

144
00:08:25,388 --> 00:08:28,892
It's selling a warm room, a seat, a bathroom, Wi-Fi,

145
00:08:28,892 --> 00:08:32,597
and a person making your drink at seven in the morning.

146
00:08:32,597 --> 00:08:36,100
And people and places get more expensive every year.

147
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:40,553
It's also why Starbucks fights over value in other ways.

148
00:08:40,553 --> 00:08:44,342
In November 2024, it dropped the extra charge for oat

149
00:08:44,342 --> 00:08:48,345
and almond milk, which had been seventy to eighty cents.

150
00:08:48,345 --> 00:08:54,350
And now Niccol says the goal is simple: "you're going to feel like it was worth it."

151
00:08:55,450 --> 00:08:57,542
So, where does your nine dollars go?

152
00:08:57,542 --> 00:09:00,040
Less than a dollar to the cup in your hand.

153
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:02,887
Almost three dollars to the goods and the trucks.

154
00:09:02,887 --> 00:09:06,257
More than four dollars to the store, and the people in it.

155
00:09:06,257 --> 00:09:08,290
And about sixty cents to Starbucks.

156
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:12,323
When you buy a nine dollar Starbucks order, you're not really buying coffee.

157
00:09:12,323 --> 00:09:14,209
You're renting a little piece of a room,

158
00:09:14,209 --> 00:09:16,660
for a few minutes, with someone to make you a drink.

159
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:19,227
Whether that's worth it is up to you.

160
00:09:19,227 --> 00:09:21,973
If you want to see who really got paid when eggs

161
00:09:21,973 --> 00:09:24,719
hit six dollars, that video is a good next stop.

162
00:09:24,719 --> 00:09:25,806
I'm The Cost Curve.

163
00:09:25,806 --> 00:09:26,950
Thanks for watching.
